# llm_efficiency_attack

A general-purpose, white-box **efficiency (sponge) attack** toolbox for
Hugging Face language models. Given a benign text input, it searches for a
small, budget-constrained perturbation that makes the model generate
noticeably more output tokens for the same input length — inflating
latency and cost without changing what the input "looks like."

This is a refactor/generalization of **NMTSloth** ([FSE 2022](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3540250.3549102),
reference codebase: [SeekingDream/FSE22_NMTSloth](https://github.com/SeekingDream/FSE22_NMTSloth)),
originally built for a single NMT architecture, generalized here to work
with any `AutoModelForCausalLM` or `AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM` from Hugging
Face.

## Install

```bash
git clone <this repo>
cd llm_efficiency_attack
pip install -e .
```

## Usage

```python
from transformers import AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM
from llm_efficiency_attack import Attacker

model = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained("Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-de")

x = "The weather today is sunny and warm, and I plan to go for a walk in the park this afternoon."
config = {
    "max_perturbed_tokens": 3,
    "num_candidates": 30,
    "max_iterations": 6,
    "objective": "neg_eos_logprob",
    "objective_horizon": 10,
    "max_new_tokens": 64,
    "seed": 0,
    "device": "cpu",
}

attack = Attacker(model)
adv_x, logs = attack.run(x, config)

print(adv_x)
print(logs["clean_generated_tokens"], "->", logs["adv_generated_tokens"])
```

Run the same thing as a script:

```bash
python examples/quickstart.py
```

**Why a translation model for the demo, not a causal LM?** Open-ended
causal LMs (GPT-2 style) don't have a well-defined "done" point for
arbitrary prompts, so a clean input often runs to `max_new_tokens`
regardless of whether it's under attack -- there's no natural early
stopping for the attack to suppress. Translation has a well-defined
completion point, so a clean input terminates quickly and the attack has
real room to show a length increase. This is also why the NMTSloth paper
itself evaluated exclusively on NMT systems (`Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-de`
is literally one of their three eval targets) rather than open-ended
causal LMs. The `Attacker` API works identically on both model families --
this is purely about which model makes for a convincing demo.

`Attacker(model)` auto-loads a matching tokenizer via `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model.config._name_or_path)`.
If you're using a model that has no Hub path (e.g. a hand-built model in a
test), pass the tokenizer explicitly: `Attacker(model, tokenizer=tokenizer)`.

## How the attack works

Autoregressive decoding is a loop: the model keeps producing tokens until
it emits an end-of-sequence (EOS) token or hits a length cap. NMTSloth's
core insight — validated in the paper via a 1,455-NMT-system empirical
study — is that **output length, not input length, determines compute
cost**, and output length is gated by two things: a large, rarely-hit
`max_length` threshold, and a runtime-generated EOS token. So an input that
suppresses EOS probability forces the model toward the expensive threshold
instead of stopping naturally.

The paper's method has three stages (Fig. 4): find critical tokens, mutate
the input, detect degradation. This package reimplements that structure
generically for any Hugging Face model, but **with several deliberate
deviations from the paper's exact method** — documented precisely below,
since "read the paper and codebase first" means being honest about where
this diverges, not just where it matches.

### Faithful to the paper

- The central hypothesis: suppressing EOS probability delays termination
  and inflates decode steps (their I-Loops metric, Eq. 4) — this package's
  `clean_generated_tokens` / `adv_generated_tokens` comparison is exactly
  that metric.
- The three-stage structure: gradient-based critical-token identification
  → candidate-based mutation → real-generation-based degradation check.
- An iterative, one-token-per-round search under an explicit perturbation
  budget (`max_perturbed_tokens` ≈ their ε ∈ {1,2,3}).
- **Token importance score** (Eq. 2): `g_i = Σ_j ∂f(x)/∂tk_i^j` — a
  *signed* sum of the objective's partial derivatives per token, matching
  the paper exactly (`Attacker._token_importance_scores`). Positions are
  ranked by `|g_i|` since the sign alone doesn't determine the swap
  direction — that's resolved by a real forward-pass evaluation per
  candidate in `_best_swap_at_position`. (This was originally implemented
  as an unsigned L2 gradient norm, which couldn't distinguish direction;
  fixed to match the paper's formula.)

### Deliberate deviations (and why)

| Paper (Sec. 5.2) | This package | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Objective `f(x) = (1/n)Σ(p_eos_i + p_oi_i)` — averaged over the model's **actual generation length** `n`, minimizing both EOS probability *and* the model's confidence in its own greedy pick | `-log P(EOS)` summed over a fixed, short `objective_horizon` (default 8) | Backpropagating through a full, dynamically-long generation trajectory for every candidate is expensive and was out of scope for a general-purpose package meant to run on arbitrary models/lengths in a demo timeframe. Dropping the confidence term is a real simplification, not just a runtime one — a follow-up objective could add it back (see `objective.py`'s registry). |
| Candidate search (Eq. 3): linear-approximation search over the **entire vocabulary** | Cosine-similarity nearest-neighbors (`num_candidates`) with real forward-pass scoring per candidate | Full-vocabulary linear scoring is what makes the paper's version fast; evaluating each candidate with a real forward pass (what this package does) is more accurate per-candidate but restricted to a local neighborhood, so it can miss a good candidate the linear approximation would have found globally. |
| Three perturbation types: character-level, token-level, **and** structure-level (BERT-based, constituency-tree-preserving) | Token-level only | This is the largest scope cut. Character-level and structure-level perturbation are not implemented. If asked "why," the honest answer is scope/time, not a technical blocker — both could be added as new candidate-generation strategies without touching the search loop. |
| Evaluates sensitivity to beam search size (`num_beams`, RQ2.3) | Greedy decoding only, no beam search path | Not implemented. The adapter's `generate_length` calls `.generate(do_sample=False)`, which is greedy; beam search would need a `num_beams` config field and a corresponding change to how `eos_logprobs` simulates the decode trajectory. |
| Hardware-dependent metrics: real CPU/GPU latency and energy via Intel RAPL and Nvidia PyNVML | Token count only | This one I'd defend as a reasonable cut rather than a gap: token count *is* the paper's own hardware-independent I-Loops metric (Eq. 4), and it's the metric the task description explicitly asks for ("output length or estimated FLOPs/latency"). Real energy measurement would tie the package to specific hardware/OS APIs, which conflicts with "general-purpose... any Hugging Face model." |
| Section 7.2: a runtime SVM mitigation/detector trained on encoder hidden states | Not implemented | Out of scope — Task 2's required API only asks for the attack (`Attacker.run`) and an evaluation utility, not a defense mechanism. |

### Where model-specific logic lives

All architecture-specific code is isolated in `adapters.py` behind the
`ModelAdapter` interface (`encode`, `decode`, `eos_logprobs`,
`generate_length`, `embedding_matrix`). `attacker.py`, `objective.py`, and
`metrics.py` never touch a Hugging Face model or tokenizer directly — they
only call the adapter. Two adapters ship today:

- `CausalLMAdapter` — decoder-only models (GPT-2 style).
- `Seq2SeqLMAdapter` — encoder-decoder models (T5/BART style).

`build_adapter(model, tokenizer, device)` picks the right one based on
`model.config.is_encoder_decoder`. **Supporting a new architecture family
means adding one adapter subclass here — nothing else in the package
changes.**

### Where the objective lives

`objective.py` is a small registry (`{"neg_eos_logprob": neg_eos_logprob}`)
so a new attack objective (e.g. targeting a specific FLOPs estimate instead
of raw token count) can be added without touching the search loop in
`attacker.py`.

## Config reference

All hyperparameters are supplied through a single JSON-serializable dict,
validated by `AttackConfig.from_dict` before the attack runs.

| Field                    | Type  | Default            | Meaning                                                                                   |
| ------------------------ | ----- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `max_perturbed_tokens`   | int   | `3`                | Perturbation budget: max number of input tokens the attack may change.                    |
| `num_candidates`         | int   | `20`                | Candidate replacement tokens evaluated per position.                                        |
| `max_iterations`         | int   | `10`                | Greedy search rounds. Each round applies at most one swap.                                  |
| `objective`               | str   | `"neg_eos_logprob"` | Which registered objective function drives the search.                                       |
| `objective_horizon`       | int   | `8`                 | Number of decode steps the objective looks at per candidate evaluation.                    |
| `max_new_tokens`         | int   | `64`                | Cap used when actually running `.generate(...)` for the before/after cost comparison.       |
| `seed`                    | int   | `0`                 | Random seed. Same config + inputs -> same result.                                            |
| `device`                  | str   | `"cpu"`             | Torch device string (`"cpu"` or `"cuda"`).                                                    |
| `min_cosine_similarity`   | float | `0.0`               | If > 0, discard candidate replacements below this embedding cosine similarity to the original token (keeps substitutions "close" to the original word). |

Unknown fields, wrong types, or out-of-range values raise a `ValueError`
with a specific message before any model call happens.

## `logs` structure

```python
{
    "config": {...},                     # effective config, echoed
    "num_tokens_perturbed": 2,
    "perturbed_positions": [1, 4],
    "iterations": [                       # one entry per search round
        {"iteration": 0, "position": 4, "objective_before": 1.2,
         "objective_after": 3.7, "improved": True},
        ...
    ],
    "clean_generated_tokens": 12,
    "adv_generated_tokens": 31,
    "length_increase": 19,
    "length_increase_ratio": 2.58,
    "wall_clock_seconds": 0.41,
}
```

## Evaluation utility

`measure_efficiency_damage(adapter, x, adv_x, max_new_tokens)` (in
`metrics.py`) is reusable outside the attack loop — e.g. to score
adversarial examples produced elsewhere:

```python
from llm_efficiency_attack.adapters import build_adapter
from llm_efficiency_attack.metrics import measure_efficiency_damage

adapter = build_adapter(model, tokenizer, device="cpu")
damage = measure_efficiency_damage(adapter, x, adv_x, max_new_tokens=64)
print(damage.length_increase_ratio)
```

## Tests

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest tests/ -v
```

The suite uses a tiny, randomly-initialized but genuinely-real
`transformers.GPT2LMHeadModel` paired with a small in-memory word-level
tokenizer (built with the `tokenizers` library, no network access needed)
so it runs fast and offline. It covers:

- `test_config.py` — config validation (defaults, overrides, every invalid
  case raises with a clear message).
- `test_attacker_end_to_end.py` — the full `Attacker.run` path on a causal
  LM: returns well-formed `(adv_x, logs)`, respects the perturbation
  budget, is reproducible under a fixed seed, and validates config before
  doing any work.
- `test_seq2seq_adapter.py` — the same end-to-end path on a T5-style
  encoder-decoder model, confirming `build_adapter` correctly dispatches to
  `Seq2SeqLMAdapter` and that the encoder/decoder gradient + generation
  plumbing works, not just the causal-LM path.
- `test_metrics.py` — the standalone cost-metric helper.

Note: because the fixture model is randomly initialized (not trained), its
EOS probabilities are close to noise, so the length increase on that
specific fixture is not necessarily large — the tests check the mechanics
(budget respected, reproducible, well-formed output), not attack strength.
`examples/quickstart.py` uses a small real pretrained translation model
(`Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-de`) where the length effect is meaningful to
observe by eye, since translation has a well-defined stopping point that
open-ended text completion does not.

## Known limitations / scope

- **Token-level perturbation only.** The paper's character-level and
  structure-level perturbation types are not implemented.
- **No beam search support.** Only greedy decoding is exercised in the
  search and in evaluation.
- The gradient-guided search is greedy and single-position-per-round; it
  is not guaranteed to find the strongest possible perturbation within the
  budget, only a locally improving one.
- `eos_logprobs` approximates true multi-step decoding by greedily feeding
  back the argmax token at each step (rather than the model's actual
  sampling/beam-search behavior), so the objective is a proxy — the
  reported `length_increase` from real `.generate(...)` calls is the
  ground truth.
- Tested against causal (GPT-2 family) and seq2seq (T5/BART family)
  architectures on both the gradient path and the real `.generate()` path;
  models with nonstandard generation loops (e.g. non-autoregressive
  decoders) may need a new `ModelAdapter` subclass.
